INT. VETERINARY HOSPITAL - SURGERY SUITE - 2 A.M.

Stainless steel, a hush of oxygen. On the table, a golden retriever under anaesthesia, chest rising slow. A heart monitor chirps in a calm rhythm.

DR. ISADORA KEMP (40s, surgeon, mask down) scrubs at the sink. TECH PRESTON LI (20s, scrubs with cartoon dogs) prepares instruments. OWNER MR. ARTHUR BELL (70s, cardigan, trembling) watches from the window of the observation room, his palm flat against the glass.

ISADORA
Preston, ready?

PRESTON
Ready.

ISADORA
Blade.

He hands it over. She works without ceremony, a quick incision. The retriever's belly opens clean. She examines the field, brow furrowing.

PRESTON
How's it look?

ISADORA
Better than the X-ray.

PRESTON
Good.

ISADORA
(to herself)
Much better.

She reaches in. Withdraws a twisted length of blue cloth, glistening. Holds it to the light.

ISADORA (CONT'D)
(to the room)
Everyone, we found it.

The intercom crackles. Mr. Bell's voice, thin and anxious, from the observation room.

MR. BELL (O.S.)
Is that.

ISADORA
A sock, Mr. Bell.

MR. BELL (O.S.)
It's blue.

ISADORA
It is.

MR. BELL (O.S.)
It's Margaret's.

ISADORA
Margaret's sock?

MR. BELL (O.S.)
She left them. When she died. I couldn't throw them away.

Isadora stops. Preston looks at her. She lowers the sock into the tray, quietly, as if it were glass.

ISADORA
Mr. Bell, we'll clean it.

MR. BELL (O.S.)
Please.

ISADORA
Preston, put it in a bag.

PRESTON
Yes, Doctor.

ISADORA
A clean bag.

PRESTON
A very clean bag.

The monitor chirps. The retriever sighs in her sleep.

ISADORA
(to herself, almost)
Who eats a sock.

PRESTON
A dog in grief.

ISADORA
I think so, yes.

She resumes. The incision closes under her hands, stitch after stitch. Preston wipes his eyes with the back of his glove.
